          text: 'In June 1348, a merchant vessel from Gascony docked at the Dorset port of Melcombe Regis (modern Weymouth), carrying fine wines and a lethal passenger: black rats carrying fleas infected with the bacterium <em>Yersinia pestis</em>. Within weeks, the <strong>Black Death</strong> was rampaging across southern England with catastrophic velocity.<br><br>The pestilence struck in two horrifying forms: <strong>bubonic plague</strong>, which caused agonizing, egg-sized swellings (buboes) in the groin and armpits that turned black and burst with foul pus, killing within five days; and <strong>pneumonic plague</strong>, which attacked the lungs, causing victims to cough up blood and die within twenty-four hours. Lacking any scientific knowledge of microbes, people believed the plague was caused by "corrupt miasma air" or God’s divine wrath. Between 1348 and 1350, roughly <strong>30% to 45% of England’s population died</strong>—nearly two million human beings.',
