          text: 'The sudden death of millions of working people triggered an economic earthquake that shattered the foundations of feudalism. In villages across the country, unharvested crops rotted in muddy fields and cattle wandered through abandoned cottages. For surviving lords, the crisis was existential: there were suddenly far more fields than labourers to work them.<br><br>For the first time in medieval history, surviving peasant serfs realized they possessed immense economic bargaining power. They demanded higher cash wages, lower rents, and the abolition of hated villeinage duties. When lords refused, peasants simply packed their tools and walked to neighboring manors that offered double wages.<br><br>Terrified of this working-class empowerment, the aristocratic landowners in Parliament struck back. In 1351, they passed the <strong>Statute of Labourers</strong>, ordering that peasant wages be frozen at pre-plague 1346 levels and imposing prison sentences on any worker who refused to labor at the old rates. It was an impossible law that generated thirty years of simmering rural fury.',
